Do not abort edge unification when one chain cannot be merged.

What it fixes
  ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain::MergeEdges lets any exception from
  MergeSubSeq escape, so a single chain whose union edge cannot be
  constructed aborts the whole operation. The reachable case: a chain
  containing an edge that is closed on one of its faces (two pcurves
  there, e.g. an edge lying along a cylinder's parametric seam) chained
  with a plain collinear neighbour — pcurve concatenation can join the
  neighbour to only one of the two representations and Geom2dConvert
  raises "Courbes non jointives". Seen on a fillet result whose corner
  pinches onto a cylindrical boss's seam generator, with merge
  tolerances of 1e-4; the same shape unifies fine when that one chain is
  simply left split, which is what this patch does.

How it was measured
  On the parcad retainer (a disc flush on the end of a plate, blended,
  tangent to the disc's seam at one side): before, UnifySameDomain with
  linear/angular tolerance 1e-4 threw and killed the build; after, the
  failed chain is simply left split and the result is unchanged
  otherwise — BRepCheck-valid, watertight, identical volume and
  bounding box (143829.66 mm^3, 59.49 x 148.126 x 36.664). parcad has
  since also started healing the stale dual representation before
  unifying, so on that shape the chain now merges cleanly and this
  guard is no longer reached; it stays because upstream still lets any
  chain's exception abort the whole operation.

Upstream status
  Not yet offered upstream (github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT).
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKShHealing/ShapeUpgrade/ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKShHealing/ShapeUpgrade/ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain.cxx
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 // commercial license or contractual agreement.
 
 #include <ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain.hxx>
+#include <Standard_ErrorHandler.hxx>
 
 #include <BRep_Builder.hxx>
 #include <BRep_Tool.hxx>
@@ -2887,10 +2888,22 @@
     {
       continue;
     }
-    if (MergeSubSeq(SeqOfSubSeqOfEdges(i).SeqsEdges, theVFmap, UE))
+    // A chain that cannot build its union edge must not abort the whole
+    // operation: an edge closed on one of its faces carries two pcurves
+    // there, and the curve concatenation can join a neighbour to only one
+    // of them, raising for the other. Leave such a chain unmerged and
+    // carry on.
+    try
     {
-      SeqOfSubSeqOfEdges(i).UnionEdges = UE;
+      OCC_CATCH_SIGNALS
+      if (MergeSubSeq(SeqOfSubSeqOfEdges(i).SeqsEdges, theVFmap, UE))
+      {
+        SeqOfSubSeqOfEdges(i).UnionEdges = UE;
+      }
     }
+    catch (Standard_Failure const&)
+    {
+    }
   }
   return true;
 }
